Support Mac OS X 10.4, and let the player own the MMC channel

Three changes, all confined to the Darwin MMC backend.

1. IOKit/storage/IOBDMediaBSDClient.h is included but no symbol from it is
   used: the only Blu-ray specific thing in the file is the "IOBDServices"
   string handed to IOServiceMatching. That header does not exist in the 10.4u
   SDK, so the include alone was enough to keep libaacs out of the PowerPC and
   Intel-32 slices -- i.e. no AACS, no retail Blu-ray, on every Mac that shipped
   with Tiger or Leopard PowerPC.

2. Tiger has no IOBDStorageFamily at all, so a Blu-ray drive publishes itself
   under IODVDServices there instead of IOBDServices. Verified on 10.4.11 with
   an external Pioneer BD drive: the MMC user client, GET CONFIGURATION and
   READ(10) all behave exactly as they do under IOBDServices on 10.5 and 10.6,
   and the drive still reports the AACS feature (0x010D). Both nubs exist from
   10.5 on, and IOBDServices is still tried first, so nothing changes there.

3. aacs_use_external_mmc() lets the application hand over a
   SCSITaskDeviceInterface it already owns.

   SCSITaskLib refuses every command unless the caller holds exclusive access
   (measured: without ObtainExclusiveAccess, READ(10) comes back refused), and
   exclusive access has exactly one owner. On 10.4 that is a hard conflict:
   Tiger misdetects BD media as a CD with 2352 byte blocks, so the volume never
   mounts and /dev/rdiskN returns EOF at any offset -- MMC is not merely the way
   to talk to the drive, it is the only way to read the disc at all. The player
   has to own that channel, which leaves libaacs unable to open a second one.

   With an external interface libaacs skips the drive lookup, DiskArbitration,
   the unmount and the exclusive access claim, and releases none of them on
   close. get_mounted_device_from_path() is skipped too, which matters on 10.4
   where there is no mount point to derive a BSD name from.

   Note the unmount/remount dance is not merely unnecessary in that mode but
   actively harmful on 10.4: releasing exclusive access makes the OS re-probe
   the media, and since it cannot make sense of BD media it ejects the disc
   (observed twice, with ASC 0x64 ILLEGAL MODE FOR THIS TRACK in the log).

4. Two things that simply do not compile for the FSF GCC / 10.4u SDK slices,
   found by building rather than by reading:

   - SCSITaskSGElement only appears in the 10.5 SDK (IOVirtualRange on 32 bit,
     IOAddressRange on 64 bit). The 10.4u SDK has neither the typedef nor a
     64 bit target, so it gets IOVirtualRange, which is what
     SetScatterGatherEntries takes there anyway.
   - device_close() still declared "__block int rc". The dispatch_sync() block
     that needed the qualifier is gone (see the no-libdispatch patch), and
     __block is a clang extension FSF GCC rejects.

   Verified: the file now compiles clean both with powerpc-apple-darwin8-gcc
   against the 10.4u SDK and with clang against the current one.

--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -52,7 +52,14 @@
     SYS=darwin
     AC_DEFINE([USE_IOKIT], 1, [Use IOKit for MMC access])
     LDFLAGS="${LDFLAGS} -lobjc -Wl,-framework,IOKit,-framework,Cocoa,-framework,DiskArbitration"
-    SET_FEATURES="${SET_FEATURES} -D_DARWIN_C_SOURCE"
+    dnl _POSIX_C_SOURCE hides non-POSIX declarations on Mac OS X -- fsync() in
+    dnl <unistd.h>, used by src/file/file_posix.c, sits inside an
+    dnl "#ifndef _POSIX_C_SOURCE" block there. Modern SDKs undo that when
+    dnl _DARWIN_C_SOURCE is also defined, but the 10.4 SDK has no such escape
+    dnl hatch, so the build fails with an implicit declaration of fsync().
+    dnl Simply do not ask for strict POSIX visibility on Darwin (libbluray
+    dnl carries the same fix for the same reason).
+    SET_FEATURES="-D_ISOC99_SOURCE -D_DARWIN_C_SOURCE"
     ;;
   *)
     SYS="${host_os}"
